    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media from the left, centre and right uniformly stress that a deepening skills shortage is reshaping the labour market, prompting simultaneous calls for expanded vocational training, greater use of migrant workers and systematic reskilling programmes to protect the sustainability of the social security system. At the same time there is broad agreement that automation, digital platforms and the erosion of traditional collective bargaining structures are accelerating structural change, leading to widespread calls for stronger works council participation and for reforms of wage and tariff policy despite divergent political framing.",
      "controversial_points": "The clash between unions demanding higher wages and employers pushing cost cuts defines the wage-policy battleground. The debate over expanding collective bargaining versus liberalizing labor law pits solidarity concepts against market flexibility ideals. The shortage of skilled workers creates tension between calls for immigration-driven solutions and nationalist narratives linking migrants to social strain. Proposals for basic income and digital platform regulation reveal a deeper conflict between welfare state expansion and fiscal conservatism."
